    <main id="main-content" class="site-main">
      <section class="section section--hero hero case-hero case-hero--mos" aria-labelledby="mos-title" data-hero-debug="off">
        <div class="case-hero__panel case-hero__media-panel">
          <figure class="case-hero__media" data-reveal="scale">
            {% progressiveImage "/assets/figma/MOS/Hero.svg", "MOS typeface hero collage with a large white serif letter g on black", 1440, 939, "", "", "", "async", "high" %}
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="case-hero__panel case-hero__text-panel">
          <div class="case-hero__grid">
            <h1 id="mos-title" class="type-display-2xl case-hero__title">
              <span>MOS.</span>
              <span class="case-hero__title-muted">A modern take on “Garalde”, renaissance roman type</span>
            </h1>
          </div>

          <div class="case-hero__grid">
            <div class="type-heading-lg case-hero__summary">
              <p>Inspired by work of Francesco Griffo and Claude Garamond. It has humanistic proportions, weighed forms and calm representation. Due to the reduced contrast and sufficient x-height suitable for a paragraph text, but a number of sharp decisions the typeface opens up his character in display sizes.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="case-content-shell" aria-label="Case content">
        <div class="case-content">
          <section class="case-text-block" aria-label="MOS project context">
            <div class="type-body-lg case-prose">
              <p>Graduated from Bolditalic course.</p>
              <p>Mos has only one master (regular, 300), Latin (52 glyphs) and Cyrillic (68 glyphs) set, numbers, and some other basic non-alphabet signs. I spent about 200 hours on it. The font has raw sidebearings and no kerning.</p>
            </div>
          </section>

